1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,553
Does a 500 rupees a month SIP really build anything?

2
00:00:03,553 --> 00:00:06,136
My brother asked me this exact question last week.

3
00:00:06,136 --> 00:00:08,720
The SIP app was open on his phone.

4
00:00:08,720 --> 00:00:12,272
On the screen, the money was exactly where it started. And he said, bhaiya,

5
00:00:12,272 --> 00:00:14,210
I'm going to stop it.

6
00:00:14,210 --> 00:00:17,395
Bhai started this SIP in June 2024.

7
00:00:17,395 --> 00:00:20,898
Every month, 500 rupees was cut automatically from the bank.

8
00:00:20,898 --> 00:00:23,446
He thought, it's a small amount, I won't feel it.

9
00:00:23,446 --> 00:00:26,949
And slowly something big will build up. Everyone thinks this way,

10
00:00:26,949 --> 00:00:28,860
and this thinking is not wrong.

11
00:00:28,860 --> 00:00:32,553
At dinner, Papa took out the FD receipts. He said,

12
00:00:32,553 --> 00:00:35,137
I told you, FD is safe.

13
00:00:35,137 --> 00:00:37,353
In the share market, money gets lost.

14
00:00:37,353 --> 00:00:39,938
Mummy was quietly listening, holding tea.

15
00:00:39,938 --> 00:00:43,630
On her face was the same old worry: what if the money is gone?

16
00:00:43,630 --> 00:00:47,023
My sister looked up from her phone and asked straight out: Bhai,

17
00:00:47,023 --> 00:00:49,490
do you even know what an SIP is?

18
00:00:49,490 --> 00:00:52,574
Or did you just hear it in some video and start?

19
00:00:52,574 --> 00:00:54,733
Bhai went quiet. The truth is,

20
00:00:54,733 --> 00:00:57,200
most people start an SIP without understanding it.

21
00:00:57,200 --> 00:00:57,897
So let's talk about

22
00:00:57,897 --> 00:01:01,384
that same dinner table today.

23
00:01:01,384 --> 00:01:03,127
Today there are three questions. First,

24
00:01:03,127 --> 00:01:06,963
how does a 500 rupees SIP actually work? Then,

25
00:01:06,963 --> 00:01:10,798
what happens if the market doesn't go straight up? And finally,

26
00:01:10,798 --> 00:01:12,890
who quietly eats up your money?

27
00:01:12,890 --> 00:01:15,480
First, the simple part: what is an SIP.

28
00:01:15,480 --> 00:01:19,550
SIP means Systematic Investment Plan. It means that every month on a fixed date,

29
00:01:19,550 --> 00:01:22,140
a fixed amount goes into a mutual fund.

30
00:01:22,140 --> 00:01:25,840
A mutual fund means the money of many people in one place.

31
00:01:25,840 --> 00:01:29,540
A professional fund manager invests it in shares or bonds.

32
00:01:29,540 --> 00:01:33,471
And the best part is, you don't have to start with a big amount.

33
00:01:33,471 --> 00:01:38,117
According to AMFI, an SIP can start from as little as 500 rupees a month.

34
00:01:38,117 --> 00:01:41,334
That's just skipping one pizza from outside in a month.

35
00:01:41,334 --> 00:01:44,550
You don't need to be rich, you need to start.

36
00:01:44,550 --> 00:01:47,994
But this is where people make the first mistake. They think,

37
00:01:47,994 --> 00:01:51,437
SIP is a scheme that gives a fixed return. No.

38
00:01:51,437 --> 00:01:53,848
SIP is only a way of putting money in.

39
00:01:53,848 --> 00:01:57,292
The return comes from the fund your money goes into.

40
00:01:57,292 --> 00:02:01,080
And the fund's return goes up and down with the market.

41
00:02:01,080 --> 00:02:05,349
Now, the first question: how do these 500 rupees work?

42
00:02:05,349 --> 00:02:10,045
There's a heavy term for it: rupee cost averaging. Don't worry,

43
00:02:10,045 --> 00:02:12,180
it means something very simple.

44
00:02:12,180 --> 00:02:15,168
Let's understand it at the vegetable market,

45
00:02:15,168 --> 00:02:17,730
where the wife goes every week.

46
00:02:17,730 --> 00:02:19,413
Suppose tomatoes are cheap,

47
00:02:19,413 --> 00:02:23,115
then you get more kilos for the same money. When tomatoes are expensive,

48
00:02:23,115 --> 00:02:24,462
you get fewer.

49
00:02:24,462 --> 00:02:27,828
If you buy tomatoes with the same amount of money every week,

50
00:02:27,828 --> 00:02:30,520
what will happen? Sometimes you'll get more, sometimes less.

51
00:02:30,520 --> 00:02:32,540
The average price will come out somewhere in the middle.

52
00:02:32,540 --> 00:02:34,677
The same thing happens in a mutual fund.

53
00:02:34,677 --> 00:02:37,731
The fund's price changes every day, it's called NAV.

54
00:02:37,731 --> 00:02:41,090
NAV means the price of one unit of the fund on that day.

55
00:02:41,090 --> 00:02:43,533
However many units your 500 rupees buy,

56
00:02:43,533 --> 00:02:45,670
that many get added to your account.

57
00:02:45,670 --> 00:02:47,905
When the market is up, NAV is expensive,

58
00:02:47,905 --> 00:02:50,779
so you get fewer units. When the market is down,

59
00:02:50,779 --> 00:02:53,333
NAV is cheap, so you get more units.

60
00:02:53,333 --> 00:02:56,526
You don't have to do anything. The money is deducted from your bank,

61
00:02:56,526 --> 00:02:59,080
and the system averages it out by itself.

62
00:02:59,080 --> 00:03:00,810
Now look at Bhai's mistake.

63
00:03:00,810 --> 00:03:04,270
He only looked at the total value on the screen. The value hadn't gone up,

64
00:03:04,270 --> 00:03:06,346
so he thought he had lost money.

65
00:03:06,346 --> 00:03:10,152
But he didn't see that in the falling market, his 500

66
00:03:10,152 --> 00:03:12,228
rupees were buying more units.

67
00:03:12,228 --> 00:03:14,650
Those very units are the real treasure for later.

68
00:03:14,650 --> 00:03:15,600
That's why they say,

69
00:03:15,600 --> 00:03:18,766
for someone doing a SIP, a falling market is not an enemy.

70
00:03:18,766 --> 00:03:20,349
It's a sale season.

71
00:03:20,349 --> 00:03:23,831
When a shop has a sale, we rush to it.

72
00:03:23,831 --> 00:03:27,630
When the share market has a sale, we rush out.

73
00:03:27,630 --> 00:03:28,580
Isn't that backwards?

74
00:03:28,580 --> 00:03:30,955
But listen to one honest thing too.

75
00:03:30,955 --> 00:03:33,669
Rupee cost averaging doesn't make losses disappear.

76
00:03:33,669 --> 00:03:36,723
If the market stays down for a very long time,

77
00:03:36,723 --> 00:03:38,759
even averaging can't save you.

78
00:03:38,759 --> 00:03:42,830
All it does is that you don't have to find the right time.

79
00:03:42,830 --> 00:03:46,923
Nobody knows the right time for the market. Not the TV people,

80
00:03:46,923 --> 00:03:51,016
not the friend who gives tips. This is called market timing,

81
00:03:51,016 --> 00:03:54,291
That means buying at the very bottom and selling at the very top.

82
00:03:54,291 --> 00:03:57,156
Even big players fail at this.

83
00:03:57,156 --> 00:04:00,840
SIP takes you out of this game.

84
00:04:00,840 --> 00:04:01,905
Now the second question,

85
00:04:01,905 --> 00:04:05,099
the one that was bothering Bhai the most.

86
00:04:05,099 --> 00:04:09,002
What if the market doesn't go straight up, and just keeps moving around the same level?

87
00:04:09,002 --> 00:04:11,487
This question isn't only Bhai's.

88
00:04:11,487 --> 00:04:14,681
Many SIP investors are thinking the same thing today.

89
00:04:14,681 --> 00:04:16,810
First, let's see what actually happened.

90
00:04:16,810 --> 00:04:21,297
Value Research looked at the data of the Nifty Total Return Index.

91
00:04:21,297 --> 00:04:25,375
Total Return means the price plus the dividends added in.

92
00:04:25,375 --> 00:04:29,862
From June 2024 to June 2026, it gave only 2.2 percent.

93
00:04:29,862 --> 00:04:32,309
Over such a long time, just this much.

94
00:04:32,309 --> 00:04:35,980
Bhai's entire SIP period went through this.

95
00:04:35,980 --> 00:04:38,519
And in 2026, by the end of June,

96
00:04:38,519 --> 00:04:40,333
the index was down 8.1 percent.

97
00:04:40,333 --> 00:04:44,686
That means since Bhai started, the market has stayed almost in the same place.

98
00:04:44,686 --> 00:04:47,226
On top of that, it fell further this year.

99
00:04:47,226 --> 00:04:50,853
What Bhai saw on his screen was not a lie.

100
00:04:50,853 --> 00:04:53,030
The market really was tired.

101
00:04:53,030 --> 00:04:55,927
Such a time is called a flat or sideways market.

102
00:04:55,927 --> 00:04:58,823
That means the market neither goes up nor goes down much,

103
00:04:58,823 --> 00:05:02,363
it just moves around here and there. This is the most boring time.

104
00:05:02,363 --> 00:05:06,225
And it's in boring times that people stop their SIPs the most.

105
00:05:06,225 --> 00:05:08,800
The mind says, nothing is happening at all.

106
00:05:08,800 --> 00:05:11,153
Bhai's logic was the same. He said,

107
00:05:11,153 --> 00:05:14,095
when I'm getting nothing, why should I put in money?

108
00:05:14,095 --> 00:05:17,037
Better I put it in an FD, and Papa will be happy too.

109
00:05:17,037 --> 00:05:19,391
Hearing it, this logic sounds completely right.

110
00:05:19,391 --> 00:05:21,450
But there is a hidden mistake in it.

111
00:05:21,450 --> 00:05:26,470
The mistake is that even in a flat market, your 500 rupees are not standing still.

112
00:05:26,470 --> 00:05:30,414
They are buying units, and when prices fall, more units.

113
00:05:30,414 --> 00:05:32,924
If and when the market moves ahead,

114
00:05:32,924 --> 00:05:35,793
these cheap units are the ones that help the most.

115
00:05:35,793 --> 00:05:39,020
Those who stop don't have them at all.

116
00:05:39,020 --> 00:05:42,016
Now understand compounding. Compounding means interest on interest,

117
00:05:42,016 --> 00:05:43,514
or return on return.

118
00:05:43,514 --> 00:05:48,758
It works only when money stays invested for a long time without a break.

119
00:05:48,758 --> 00:05:52,128
If you stop the SIP, the chain of compounding breaks.

120
00:05:52,128 --> 00:05:54,750
And lost time never comes back.

121
00:05:54,750 --> 00:05:57,579
Imagine a boy who goes to the gym every month,

122
00:05:57,579 --> 00:06:01,470
but his body doesn't get built. After a few months he quits.

123
00:06:01,470 --> 00:06:04,299
And the month his body would have started to take shape,

124
00:06:04,299 --> 00:06:06,068
he isn't even there.

125
00:06:06,068 --> 00:06:09,958
Stopping your SIP is just like that. You went through the days of hard work,

126
00:06:09,958 --> 00:06:12,080
and on the day the results come, you're gone.

127
00:06:12,080 --> 00:06:13,959
But here too, let me be honest.

128
00:06:13,959 --> 00:06:16,966
Nobody can say when the market will pick up.

129
00:06:16,966 --> 00:06:19,973
It may stay flat for even longer.

130
00:06:19,973 --> 00:06:24,483
That's why do your SIP only with money you won't need soon.

131
00:06:24,483 --> 00:06:27,490
Then you won't have to sell in a falling market.

132
00:06:27,490 --> 00:06:31,192
Now the third question. Who is quietly eating your money?

133
00:06:31,192 --> 00:06:33,414
My sister asked exactly this. Brother,

134
00:06:33,414 --> 00:06:37,116
does the fund manager work for free? Not at all.

135
00:06:37,116 --> 00:06:39,708
His fee is called the expense ratio.

136
00:06:39,708 --> 00:06:42,300
And this fee is charged every year.

137
00:06:42,300 --> 00:06:45,271
Expense ratio means the yearly cost of running the fund.

138
00:06:45,271 --> 00:06:48,985
The fund manager's salary, office, research, it all comes into this.

139
00:06:48,985 --> 00:06:51,956
It doesn't come separately in your bill.

140
00:06:51,956 --> 00:06:55,299
It gets quietly deducted from the fund's value.

141
00:06:55,299 --> 00:06:58,270
That's why most people never even look at it.

142
00:06:58,270 --> 00:06:59,296
Think of a kirana shop.

143
00:06:59,296 --> 00:07:02,375
The shopkeeper keeps a small margin on every item.

144
00:07:02,375 --> 00:07:04,427
On one item you don't notice it.

145
00:07:04,427 --> 00:07:06,479
But add up the whole year's account,

146
00:07:06,479 --> 00:07:08,531
and it becomes a good amount of money.

147
00:07:08,531 --> 00:07:11,610
Expense ratio is the same, it looks small,

148
00:07:11,610 --> 00:07:13,320
but it is charged every year.

149
00:07:13,320 --> 00:07:16,186
And it is charged on your entire saved money,

150
00:07:16,186 --> 00:07:18,097
not just on the new 500 rupees.

151
00:07:18,097 --> 00:07:20,008
As the corpus grows bigger,

152
00:07:20,008 --> 00:07:21,919
the fees also keep growing bigger.

153
00:07:21,919 --> 00:07:24,785
Corpus means your total saved money, along with returns.

154
00:07:24,785 --> 00:07:27,970
Over a long time, this small difference becomes big.

155
00:07:27,970 --> 00:07:29,231
Now the useful part.

156
00:07:29,231 --> 00:07:32,068
Every fund has a direct plan and a regular plan.

157
00:07:32,068 --> 00:07:35,220
In a regular plan there is an agent or distributor in between,

158
00:07:35,220 --> 00:07:37,111
and his commission gets added too.

159
00:07:37,111 --> 00:07:39,318
In a direct plan there is no such commission.

160
00:07:39,318 --> 00:07:43,100
That's why the expense ratio of a direct plan is usually lower.

161
00:07:43,100 --> 00:07:45,038
And there's one more hidden cost: tax.

162
00:07:45,038 --> 00:07:47,299
If you hold an equity fund for more than a year,

163
00:07:47,299 --> 00:07:50,207
the profit is called long term capital gain.

164
00:07:50,207 --> 00:07:52,145
12.5 percent tax is charged on this.

165
00:07:52,145 --> 00:07:56,990
But every year, such profit up to 1.25 lakh rupees is exempt from tax.

166
00:07:56,990 --> 00:08:01,647
Someone with a 500 rupee SIP will hardly ever have to pay this tax in the beginning.

167
00:08:01,647 --> 00:08:04,513
But keep it in mind. When you look at returns, always think:

168
00:08:04,513 --> 00:08:07,737
what will be left in your hand after costs and tax.

169
00:08:07,737 --> 00:08:11,320
That is the real return. Everything else is just screen shine.

170
00:08:11,320 --> 00:08:14,267
Now, back to the dinner table. Brother asked,

171
00:08:14,267 --> 00:08:16,478
so is FD useless? No.

172
00:08:16,478 --> 00:08:20,162
Papa's FD has its own place. Emergency money,

173
00:08:20,162 --> 00:08:22,741
money you need quickly, is fine there.

174
00:08:22,741 --> 00:08:27,530
Bank deposits also come with insurance cover of up to 5 lakh rupees.

175
00:08:27,530 --> 00:08:31,015
The biggest mistake is making decisions out of fear. The market falls,

176
00:08:31,015 --> 00:08:35,274
you see a red screen on the news, and the SIP is stopped. Then the market recovers,

177
00:08:35,274 --> 00:08:38,759
and people come back at the higher price.

178
00:08:38,759 --> 00:08:41,082
That means they left it when it was cheap and bought when it was expensive.

179
00:08:41,082 --> 00:08:44,180
This is the thing that spoils SIP the most.

180
00:08:44,180 --> 00:08:48,102
Another mistake is picking a fund by looking only at last year's return.

181
00:08:48,102 --> 00:08:50,597
The fund that was at the top last year

182
00:08:50,597 --> 00:08:54,519
won't necessarily stay there. And one more mistake: opening the app every day.

183
00:08:54,519 --> 00:08:58,084
If you open it every day, you'll feel afraid every day. SIP is a long race,

184
00:08:58,084 --> 00:08:59,510
not a daily score.

185
00:08:59,510 --> 00:09:02,247
So what should you do? First an emergency fund, then SIP.

186
00:09:02,247 --> 00:09:05,669
Keep the SIP at an amount that can continue even in a bad month,

187
00:09:05,669 --> 00:09:08,064
even if it is just 500 rupees.

188
00:09:08,064 --> 00:09:10,459
Check for a direct plan and the expense ratio.

189
00:09:10,459 --> 00:09:13,880
And look at your accounts just a few times a year, not every day.

190
00:09:13,880 --> 00:09:16,911
And when your salary goes up, increase your SIP a little too.

191
00:09:16,911 --> 00:09:18,596
This is called a step-up SIP,

192
00:09:18,596 --> 00:09:21,964
meaning increasing the SIP amount every year. Brother said,

193
00:09:21,964 --> 00:09:23,985
Okay bhaiya, I won't stop it.

194
00:09:23,985 --> 00:09:27,690
Papa kept the FD receipt and gave a faint smile.

195
00:09:27,690 --> 00:09:29,364
Let me make one thing clear.

196
00:09:29,364 --> 00:09:32,043
I am not naming any fund,

197
00:09:32,043 --> 00:09:34,722
and I am not promising any return.

198
00:09:34,722 --> 00:09:37,067
The market goes up, and it goes down too.

199
00:09:37,067 --> 00:09:40,080
This is not investment advice. Look at your own needs,

200
00:09:40,080 --> 00:09:41,420
and make your decision wisely.

201
00:09:41,420 --> 00:09:45,081
In the next video, we'll talk about PPF and EPF,

202
00:09:45,081 --> 00:09:47,521
which Papa likes so much.

203
00:09:47,521 --> 00:09:50,775
Are they really better than SIP?

204
00:09:50,775 --> 00:09:52,809
But before that, a question for you.

205
00:09:52,809 --> 00:09:56,470
Have you ever stopped your SIP when the market fell?

206
00:09:56,470 --> 00:09:58,910
Yes or no, write in the comments.
